\title[Rigidity of the Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle]{Rigidity of the Donoho--Stark Uncertainty Principle on Finite Abelian Groups}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group of order $N$ and let $f:G\to\C$ be nonzero. The Donoho--Stark
uncertainty principle asserts that $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\dual f|\ge N$. We give a complete
solution of the associated extremal problem: equality holds if and only if
$f=c\,\chi\,\one_{a+H}$ for some subgroup $H\le G$, character $\chi\in\dual G$, base point
$a\in G$ and scalar $c\ne0$ --- a \emph{modulated coset indicator}. The proof is a four-stage
equality analysis: Plancherel combined with Cauchy--Schwarz forces $|\dual f|$ to be constant on
its support with value $\|f\|_1$; the equality case of the triangle inequality forces the
demodulated values $\overline{\psi(x)}f(x)$ to be positively proportional; Fourier inversion
converts this into constancy of $|f|$ on $\supp f$; and the resulting simultaneous phase
coherence across the entire spectrum produces a \emph{phase subgroup} $H$ whose annihilator
duality $|H|\cdot|H^{\perp}|=N$ closes a counting argument, pinning $\supp f$ to exactly one
coset of $H$.

We develop the consequences: explicit transforms and self-duality of the extremal family; the
sharp value distribution $|f(x)|=\|f\|_1/|\supp f|$; intrinsic cosetness of the support;
invariance of extremality under the Fourier transform; the divisibility obstruction
$|\supp f|\mid N$ and its sharpness; a prime-order dichotomy; canonicity of the phase subgroup as
the group of periods of the support; uniqueness of the classification data and an orbit
description of the extremal set; the combinatorial corollary that an indicator function is
extremal precisely when the set is a coset; and a discrete gap theorem: if $|\supp f|$ divides
$N$ and $f$ is not a modulated coset indicator, then
$|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\dual f|\ge N+|\supp f|$.

Finally we exhibit an adversarial construction showing that the two ``flatness'' conditions
extracted from the rigidity proof do \emph{not} characterise extremality. On the self-dual group
$G=K\times\dual K$ the evaluation pairing $f(x,\psi)=\psi(x)$ --- the discrete chirp --- satisfies
$|f|\equiv1$ on $G$ and $|\dual f|\equiv|K|=\sqrt{|G|}$ on $\dual G$, yet
$|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\dual f|=|G|^2$, the maximum possible value. Rigidity is therefore a
statement about inter-spectral phase coupling, not about magnitudes.
\end{abstract}

